1
00:00:00,570 --> 00:00:02,590
Hello class, this lecture will

2
00:00:02,590 --> 00:00:05,190
focus on antiarrhythmic drugs.

3
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:11,990
So in this lecture, I will
begin with a brief review

4
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:16,230
of the action potential,
because many of these drugs

5
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:20,630
will affect either
depolarization or repolarization.

6
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:25,410
I will also consider the
adverse effects, because

7
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:30,170
as you will come to
see, some of these drugs

8
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:34,470
themselves can be pro
-arrhythmic. So it may be

9
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:38,850
necessary to evaluate
the patient and assess do

10
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:43,290
they need medication or
can this issue be managed

11
00:00:43,290 --> 00:00:48,190
with a pacemaker or
ablation or cardioversion.

12
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:53,910
And then I will also discuss
some of the miscellaneous

13
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,830
medications that we use
for arrhythmias. And among

14
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:05,570
them will be drugs like
digoxin or adenosine, or

15
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:09,710
even giving ions themselves,
whether it be potassium

16
00:01:09,710 --> 00:01:15,090
or even magnesium for
torsades or digoxin toxicity.

17
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:21,990
So, with the action
potential, you can see on the

18
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:27,890
left side, where we begin
with the upstroke from phase

19
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:32,430
0 to 1, that is the opening
of the sodium channels,

20
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:37,010
and you will now have the
influx of positive charge

21
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:43,110
causing depolarization. So
the drugs that are considered

22
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:47,970
to be sodium channel blockers
will have some impact

23
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:52,390
on depolarization,
especially the 1A and the 1C.

24
00:01:53,210 --> 00:02:00,330
When we have the sodium
entering the cell, the

25
00:02:00,330 --> 00:02:05,250
channels will become
inactivated. They'll close,

26
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:08,890
and there we will begin
initial repolarization.

27
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:15,030
At phase 2, we will begin
now to have an opening

28
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:19,680
of the calcium channel,
and that allows for

29
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:23,610
calcium entry, and that is
really where we have the

30
00:02:23,610 --> 00:02:26,930
plateau or the shoulder
of the action potential.

31
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:30,370
And the drugs that we know as

32
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:33,430
class 4 calcium
channel blockers,

33
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:37,750
rapamil and dultiazem,
will work in this area.

34
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:43,590
So in phase three,
the calcium channels

35
00:02:43,590 --> 00:02:45,650
begin to close, and now we have

36
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:48,010
the opening of the
potassium channel.

37
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:50,670
And now potassium takes over,

38
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:52,260
and we get repolarization.

39
00:02:52,330 --> 00:02:55,370
And this now should
make sense because

40
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:58,930
repolarization,
depending on potassium,

41
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:03,630
would mean sort of
the rest for the cell.

42
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:08,750
Okay, potassium is the ion
of rest. And then phase

43
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:13,870
4, which really corresponds
a lot to automaticity

44
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:19,290
and will be the site of
action in the pacemaker.

45
00:03:19,290 --> 00:03:22,130
And drugs like beta
blockers and calcium

46
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:24,090
channel blockers
fill nicely here

47
00:03:24,090 --> 00:03:27,350
because they reduce
the slope of phase 4.

48
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:32,450
What you start to
get is a reset. You

49
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:35,030
start to reset all
the ions, sodium,

50
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:37,870
calcium, and potassium.

51
00:03:37,870 --> 00:03:41,990
And that is achieved
through the anteport

52
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:46,890
mechanism that involves
the sodium and calcium

53
00:03:46,890 --> 00:03:49,290
exchanger. And remember,
with that exchange

54
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:53,450
mechanism, for every
three sodium that comes

55
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:56,810
back in, one calcium
will come out. Now,

56
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:04,230
EKG is important
because we know that

57
00:04:04,230 --> 00:04:06,950
these drugs, given
their mechanism, will

58
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:12,850
have subtle changes
shown on the EKG.

59
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:19,230
So, the P wave corresponds
to the atrium, and you'll

60
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:23,230
see that this might be
changed in the situation of

61
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:26,350
maybe an atrial fibrillation
or an atrial flutter.

62
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:32,730
So the PR interval
corresponds to the movement

63
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:36,210
of the action potential
from the atria through

64
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:40,430
the AV node. And the AV
node is very important.

65
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:43,630
That's the atrial ventricular
node. And I like to

66
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:49,250
say that this AV node
is almost like a filter.

67
00:04:49,770 --> 00:04:55,450
It slows the impulse.
without the AV node, you

68
00:04:55,450 --> 00:05:00,210
probably will see patients
having heart rates upward

69
00:05:00,210 --> 00:05:03,090
into the three or four
hundreds. So if there's

70
00:05:03,090 --> 00:05:07,350
an issue with the AV node,
that is a big problem.

71
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:13,550
QRS is the ventricular
depolarization. So when I

72
00:05:13,550 --> 00:05:19,850
think of that, I think of
sodium. And the QRS duration

73
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:24,290
really stands for the fact
where all the ventricular

74
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:28,170
cells become activated.
And this will be important

75
00:05:28,170 --> 00:05:33,130
to evaluate when we do the
sodium channel blockers.

76
00:05:33,130 --> 00:05:38,990
The T wave represents
ventricular repolarization.

77
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:43,770
So you have to associate the
T wave and repolarization

78
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:47,570
somewhat with potassium.
and it makes sense.

79
00:05:47,730 --> 00:05:52,050
Patients that have
hypokalemia, maybe

80
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:53,950
due to the fact
that they're taking

81
00:05:53,950 --> 00:05:58,250
diuretics like
thiazides or loops,

82
00:05:58,650 --> 00:06:03,150
they will probably
have, in low potassium

83
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:06,710
states, slower repolarization.
And that really

84
00:06:06,710 --> 00:06:11,370
shows as a decreased
T-wave on the EKG.

85
00:06:12,490 --> 00:06:18,630
with hyperkalemia which is
attributed to ACE inhibitors

86
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:22,510
ARBs and maybe spironolactone
you're going to

87
00:06:22,510 --> 00:06:26,110
get a slowering of
conduction and a decrease in

88
00:06:26,110 --> 00:06:31,810
action potential duration
and that sometimes on an EKG

89
00:06:31,810 --> 00:06:38,490
will show as a peaked T
wave and then lastly QT is

90
00:06:38,490 --> 00:06:41,990
the duration of the
ventricular action potential.

91
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:48,150
Increase in QT can happen
with medications, especially

92
00:06:48,150 --> 00:06:53,270
if we're prolonging
repolarization, and that has

93
00:06:53,270 --> 00:06:57,970
to do with some degree of
potassium channel blockade.

94
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:03,750
You'll see this happening
with the drugs of the 1A

95
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:07,250
antiarrhythmic class, and
you'll also see it to an

96
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:12,770
extent with the class 3
potassium channel blockers.

97
00:07:12,770 --> 00:07:17,750
The other time that QT
can be an issue is, of

98
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:23,510
course, those people that
have a genetic long QT. In

99
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:26,830
those cases, they need to
get that resolved somehow,

100
00:07:26,830 --> 00:07:30,870
whether it be through
ablation or through another

101
00:07:30,870 --> 00:07:35,750
type of method. And
remember, when we talk about

102
00:07:35,750 --> 00:07:39,870
this idea of ventricular
tachycardia, ventricular

103
00:07:39,870 --> 00:07:43,490
fibrillation, they're
both serious, but V-fib is

104
00:07:43,490 --> 00:07:47,510
probably the worst, where the
impulses become so chaotic

105
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:50,750
that the patient can end
up in cardiac arrest.

106
00:07:52,370 --> 00:07:56,850
There are three main mechanisms
of arrhythmias. Number

107
00:07:56,850 --> 00:08:02,170
one is going to be an increase
in automaticity. And I

108
00:08:02,170 --> 00:08:07,270
tend to think that this is
coming from the pacemaker.

109
00:08:08,730 --> 00:08:12,980
Mostly we see the
issue happening in

110
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:16,190
phase four of the
action potential.

111
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:20,930
What I will say here,
this is usually,

112
00:08:20,930 --> 00:08:25,420
what's usually involved
is norepinepirin

113
00:08:25,690 --> 00:08:28,630
binding to the beta
receptors in the heart.

114
00:08:29,230 --> 00:08:32,510
and when you have that
happening of course you're

115
00:08:32,510 --> 00:08:36,150
going to get an increase in
heart rate from the pacemaker

116
00:08:36,150 --> 00:08:40,690
and you will also have
some degree of AV node

117
00:08:40,690 --> 00:08:45,590
enhancement. So when you give
the patient whether a beta

118
00:08:45,590 --> 00:08:50,430
blocker or you give a calcium
channel blocker such as

119
00:08:50,430 --> 00:08:56,630
rapamil and you will decrease
the slope of phase 4.

120
00:08:56,630 --> 00:09:00,750
And that will change now
the whole idea of your rate.

121
00:09:01,690 --> 00:09:07,810
Triggered, the
most likely reasons

122
00:09:07,810 --> 00:09:09,650
for this type of arrhythmia

123
00:09:09,650 --> 00:09:16,330
is either from an early
after depolarization, which

124
00:09:16,330 --> 00:09:22,570
tends to happen around phase
3 of the action potential.

125
00:09:22,570 --> 00:09:28,390
I will say that the most
common reasons for this is

126
00:09:28,390 --> 00:09:33,010
a situation of hypokalemia
and a lowered heart rate.

127
00:09:34,170 --> 00:09:41,190
Delayed after depolarization
happens right here, after

128
00:09:41,190 --> 00:09:44,630
phase 4 of the action
potential. And we tend to

129
00:09:44,630 --> 00:09:49,810
see that the mechanism here
is high levels of calcium

130
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:54,250
influx. It can happen
through that because maybe of

131
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:57,250
a digoxin toxicity, and
you'll see a little bit later,

132
00:09:57,250 --> 00:10:01,390
or those patients who have
had an ischemic event.

133
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:07,310
And the reentry is
pretty straightforward in

134
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:12,090
explanation. is that the
impulse goes through the

135
00:10:12,090 --> 00:10:16,470
heart and it excites the
pathway and then for some

136
00:10:16,470 --> 00:10:20,490
reason it goes back and
re-excites that pathway.

137
00:10:21,490 --> 00:10:24,930
Probably the best example
would be the situation

138
00:10:24,930 --> 00:10:28,690
of atrial fibrillation
or atrial flutter.

139
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:33,930
But there's also certain
heart conditions like Wolf

140
00:10:33,930 --> 00:10:37,270
-Parkinson's White where
there may be the formation

141
00:10:37,270 --> 00:10:40,870
of an accessory pathway,
such as the bundle of Kent.

142
00:10:40,870 --> 00:10:45,230
So we'll talk about the
medical management of

143
00:10:45,230 --> 00:10:48,190
Wolff-Parkinson's White
at the end of the lecture.

144
00:10:50,070 --> 00:10:54,010
So this is the classification
of antiarrhythmic

145
00:10:54,010 --> 00:10:57,490
drugs that is in the textbook.
It's known as the Vaughan

146
00:10:57,490 --> 00:11:01,030
-Williams classification,
where there is four

147
00:11:01,030 --> 00:11:05,290
major classes and then
miscellaneous. and that would

148
00:11:05,290 --> 00:11:09,810
include drugs like the
doxin, adenosine, magnesium.

149
00:11:13,750 --> 00:11:19,770
Class 1 antiarrhythmics
will all block the sodium

150
00:11:19,770 --> 00:11:25,330
channels. So there will be,
especially with the 1A and

151
00:11:25,330 --> 00:11:30,890
the 1C, a definite change
with depolarization. Okay,

152
00:11:31,230 --> 00:11:36,770
so 1A, as you can see, you're
getting some prolongation

153
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:41,370
of depolarization, and
that has to do with

154
00:11:41,370 --> 00:11:45,930
blockage of the sodium
channels, and that would be

155
00:11:45,930 --> 00:11:51,190
attributed now on the EKG
to an increase in QRS.

156
00:11:52,630 --> 00:12:00,070
1A also blocks potassium
channels and that would

157
00:12:00,070 --> 00:12:04,770
mean that you are prolonging
as you can see here

158
00:12:04,770 --> 00:12:08,130
repolarization as you can
see in the red line and

159
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:12,790
that will increase QT
so that is one mechanism

160
00:12:12,790 --> 00:12:18,210
by which the 1As themselves
can be proarrhythmic

161
00:12:18,330 --> 00:12:21,430
The other thing is that
the three drugs in this

162
00:12:21,430 --> 00:12:26,750
group do have muscarinic
receptor blockade.

163
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:29,550
M2 receptors are in the heart.

164
00:12:29,550 --> 00:12:32,050
If you block those receptors,

165
00:12:32,050 --> 00:12:34,210
you're turning on sympathetics.

166
00:12:34,550 --> 00:12:38,070
And what you're going to
see is an increase in AV

167
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:43,060
node action. Okay, so with
many of them, especially

168
00:12:43,060 --> 00:12:46,990
disopiramide, you will
likely have to pair that

169
00:12:46,990 --> 00:12:50,490
up with an AV node suppressant
like a beta blocker.

170
00:12:52,050 --> 00:12:58,870
1B is interesting
in that you're going

171
00:12:58,870 --> 00:13:02,310
to not have much going on with

172
00:13:02,310 --> 00:13:05,210
depolarization, as you
can see in this line.

173
00:13:05,730 --> 00:13:11,650
and that makes sense because
1B drugs like lidocaine

174
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:16,950
are given in situations
where the tissue has

175
00:13:16,950 --> 00:13:21,130
already been depolarized
and the biggest example of

176
00:13:21,130 --> 00:13:26,570
that is in a ischemic episode
so lidocaine is usually

177
00:13:26,570 --> 00:13:32,250
given post MI in the hours
post MI to prevent that

178
00:13:32,250 --> 00:13:35,690
patient from having a
possible ventricular event.

179
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:39,710
The other thing that makes
it so nice is, as you

180
00:13:39,710 --> 00:13:43,590
can see, there is no effect
on the potassium channels.

181
00:13:43,590 --> 00:13:47,230
As a matter of fact, as
you see, you're getting

182
00:13:47,230 --> 00:13:50,950
a shortening of the
action potential, and that

183
00:13:50,950 --> 00:13:54,930
would lower the QT, and
that makes it, again, very

184
00:13:54,980 --> 00:14:00,230
important in those patients
that have had an MI.

185
00:14:00,930 --> 00:14:03,590
In depolarized cells,

186
00:14:04,830 --> 00:14:11,170
this mechanism will
slow the conduction.

187
00:14:11,830 --> 00:14:15,150
And that's very
important post-MI.

188
00:14:16,070 --> 00:14:20,850
1C, as you can envision
here, this is the one

189
00:14:20,850 --> 00:14:25,430
with the most prolongation
of depolarization.

190
00:14:26,550 --> 00:14:29,630
And you see that
green line shifting to

191
00:14:29,630 --> 00:14:33,130
the right, more so
definitely than the 1A.

192
00:14:33,550 --> 00:14:40,370
And this group is going
to enhance QRS. And that

193
00:14:40,370 --> 00:14:43,270
itself in certain
patients can be an issue

194
00:14:43,270 --> 00:14:47,450
because if you're increasing
that interval, you're

195
00:14:47,450 --> 00:14:50,710
activating the ventricular
cells. So you need to

196
00:14:50,710 --> 00:14:55,670
give this in certain
cases, and I'll speak about

197
00:14:55,670 --> 00:14:59,940
it in a moment. But with
respect to prolonging

198
00:15:01,150 --> 00:15:05,510
the action potential or
shortening it, it doesn't.

199
00:15:05,510 --> 00:15:08,030
So as you can see,
there's no effect on QT.

200
00:15:08,250 --> 00:15:13,250
One thing I want to
bring up is last semester

201
00:15:13,250 --> 00:15:17,220
we discussed antidepressants,
the tricyclics.

202
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:20,550
And we said that they
used to be used before

203
00:15:20,550 --> 00:15:25,050
the SSRIs, but they
have autonomic effects.

204
00:15:26,690 --> 00:15:31,710
And what we are concerned
with in an overdose,

205
00:15:31,710 --> 00:15:36,380
say, of amitriptyline is
that it is cardiotoxic.

206
00:15:36,510 --> 00:15:41,090
And if you look at how
that happens, it kind of

207
00:15:41,090 --> 00:15:45,430
mimics what's happening
with the 1A sodium channel

208
00:15:45,430 --> 00:15:49,160
blockers. Those patients
on an EKG that have

209
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:53,470
overdosed will have a high
QRS and a high QT. And

210
00:15:53,470 --> 00:15:58,310
that is why, with respect
to managing an overdose

211
00:15:58,310 --> 00:16:02,770
of a tricyclic, we give
sodium bicarbonate itself

212
00:16:02,770 --> 00:16:07,490
to stabilize the sodium
channels of the heart.

213
00:16:10,590 --> 00:16:15,850
Class I drugs are use
-dependent. And this is

214
00:16:15,850 --> 00:16:19,470
an interesting concept
where you're going to

215
00:16:19,470 --> 00:16:26,770
get more of an inhibition
of depolarization on

216
00:16:26,770 --> 00:16:30,040
those cells that are
frequently depolarizing.

217
00:16:30,070 --> 00:16:33,150
That means that those
patients that have

218
00:16:33,150 --> 00:16:37,750
tachycardia or faster
heart rates are going

219
00:16:37,750 --> 00:16:40,210
to have increased
sodium channel block.

220
00:16:41,090 --> 00:16:43,970
And that is known
as use dependence.

221
00:16:46,250 --> 00:16:49,590
So, in identifying these drugs,

222
00:16:50,330 --> 00:16:53,790
First Aid puts it
nicely, and I have

223
00:16:53,790 --> 00:16:58,770
adapted it from them,
that the queen prefers

224
00:16:58,770 --> 00:17:03,150
discos, and that
would mean quinidine,

225
00:17:03,310 --> 00:17:06,590
percanamide, and disopiramide.

226
00:17:06,860 --> 00:17:13,150
So these drugs will
block the open sodium

227
00:17:13,150 --> 00:17:18,110
channels, and they're
going to slow the 0 to 1.

228
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:20,210
And you can see that the

229
00:17:20,210 --> 00:17:21,890
line is shifted to the right.

230
00:17:22,790 --> 00:17:26,550
And they're also, with the
fact that they're blocking

231
00:17:26,550 --> 00:17:31,730
potassium channels, they're
going to also prolong

232
00:17:31,730 --> 00:17:36,550
the action potential. They
also prolong the refractory

233
00:17:36,550 --> 00:17:39,970
period. Now, the refractory
period is the time

234
00:17:39,970 --> 00:17:45,320
it takes for the sodium
channels to recover from being

235
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:49,510
activated and then a new
impulse to be generated.

236
00:17:51,070 --> 00:17:55,410
These drugs will treat both

237
00:17:55,410 --> 00:17:58,870
atrial and ventricular
arrhythmias

238
00:17:59,250 --> 00:18:03,990
Due to the effect on the
sodium channels, you're

239
00:18:03,990 --> 00:18:07,310
going to get an increase
in QRS. And because of the

240
